        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.084,
            0.353,
            0.103
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Some discussion points:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.104,
            0.47,
            0.124
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Which of the spoons get hotter?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.128,
            0.743,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What does this experiment tell us about heat transfer along the two spoons?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.171,
            0.744,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You may have noticed that the metal spoon is hotter to touch than the wooden spoon. Even though both spoons are immersed in the water of the same temperature and for the same time. This shows that the heat transfers through the metal spoon, making it hotter. In contrast, the wooden spoon transfers heat poorly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.266,
            0.744,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In such cases, the transfer of heat from one point to another of a material is called conduction, and materials that transfer heat are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "conductors",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.323,
            0.744,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Based on the observations, one can say that metals are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "good conductors of heat",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", whereas wood is a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "poor conductor of heat",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Now, we can understand why mostly metal vessels are used for cooking, and their handles are made with wood or other materials that do not conduct heat. You will learn more about this in the chapter ‘Heat Transfer in Nature’."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.447,
            0.513,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4.1.5 Conduction of electricity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.475,
            0.744,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Have you ever seen an electrician using a screwdriver? What type of material is used for making its handle? You may have also noticed the electrician wearing rubber gloves and shoes while working. What can be the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "reason",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for this?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.577,
            0.501,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 4.4: Let us design and create"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.616,
            0.744,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Design",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " an electric circuit, like the ‘tester’ circuit in the chapter ‘Electricity: Circuits and their Components’. Repeat the same activity using the materials listed below and record your observations in Table 4.2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.692,
            0.744,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You may collect a few objects, such as a piece of aluminium foil, an iron nail, a lump of sulfur (pea-sized), a copper wire, a piece of coal, a piece of dry wood, a stone, an eraser made of rubber and a piece of nylon rope."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.771,
            0.743,
            0.81
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Predict",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " which of these could make the bulb of the tester glow and which could not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "aside_text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.957,
            0.119,
            0.98,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The World of Metals and Non-metals"
            }
          ]
        },
